---
import PathwayLandingLayout from '../../layouts/PathwayLandingLayout.astro';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/seo/config';
import { TEA_INVESTMENT_THRESHOLD } from '@/lib/eb5-investment';

const title = 'EB-5 for High-Net-Worth Families | StudentEB5';
const metaDescription =
  'EB-5 for high-net-worth families focuses on residency planning, children’s education timing, and comparing EB-5 to other global investment-migration options when the TEA threshold is not the binding constraint.';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_CONFIG.url}/pathways/eb5-for-wealthy-families`;
const h1 = 'EB-5 for High-Net-Worth Families';
const subhead =
  'When the capital threshold is not the hard part — and family residency, education timing, and global mobility options drive the EB-5 conversation instead.';

const teaFormatted = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', {
  style: 'currency',
  currency: 'USD',
  maximumFractionDigits: 0,
}).format(TEA_INVESTMENT_THRESHOLD);

const relatedResearch = [
  {
    title: 'Golden Visas vs EB-5 for Elite Students',
    url: '/research/golden-visas-vs-eb5-elite-students',
    description: 'Side-by-side framing of EB-5 and other investment-migration programs for student families.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Golden Visa Global Education — Student Stories',
    url: '/research/golden-visa-global-education-student-stories',
    description: 'How education goals intersect with residency and investment-migration planning.',
  },
];
---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone About EB-5 for High-Net-Worth Families"
  breadcrumbTitle="EB-5 for High-Net-Worth Families"
  keywords={[
    'EB-5 for high-net-worth families',
    'EB-5 family residency planning',
    'EB-5 vs golden visa',
    'investment migration education',
  ]}
>
  <Fragment slot="extraCompliance">
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed border border-border rounded-lg bg-secondary/40 p-4 m-0">
      EB-5 investment involves financial risk, including potential loss of principal, and does not guarantee a green
      card, visa availability, or any specific processing time. Comparisons to other residency or investment-migration
      programs are educational only and are not recommendations. Consult a qualified immigration attorney and a
      FINRA-registered financial adviser for guidance specific to your situation.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <h2>When {teaFormatted} is not the binding constraint</h2>
  <p>
    For some families, the Targeted Employment Area (TEA) investment threshold of {teaFormatted} — capital only, before
    fees and professional costs — is affordable relative to overall balance-sheet capacity. The harder questions become
    structural: where the family wants long-term residency, how multiple children's U.S. education and immigration
    clocks align, and whether a U.S. permanent-residence path should sit alongside or instead of other global
    residency or investment-migration options. This page addresses that planning layer.
  </p>

  <h2>Family residency and multi-child timing</h2>
  <p>
    EB-5 can include a spouse and unmarried children under 21 as derivatives on the investor's petition when eligibility
    rules are met. High-net-worth households often evaluate filing timing against several academic calendars at once —
    staggered graduations, OPT windows, and aging-out risk for older children — rather than treating EB-5 as a single
    adult's career move. Those interactions are case-specific; attorney review is required before assuming any child
    remains eligible as a derivative.
  </p>

  <h2>Comparing EB-5 to other global options</h2>
  <p>
    Some families also review European or other investment-migration and “golden visa” style programs while considering
    U.S. EB-5. Those programs differ in rights conferred, physical-presence rules, path to permanent residence or
    citizenship, due diligence standards, and family coverage. This site does not recommend one over another. For
    educational comparison framing aimed at student families, see
    <a href="/research/golden-visas-vs-eb5-elite-students">golden visas vs EB-5 for elite students</a> and
    <a href="/research/golden-visa-global-education-student-stories">golden visa global education student stories</a>.
  </p>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
